Softlanding, founded in 1992, predated and grew into Slackware;
according to Wikipedia, Debian was founded as a reaction to
Softlanding.  Debian was founded in August 1993, with version 0.91
released in January 1994, but no public releases followed until
0.93R5, in March 1995, called by the author of "A Brief History of
Debian" the first modern release.

Even at that time, there were a number of ways to get Linux, with
Softlanding and bootkit/rootkit (what we'd probably call Linux from
Scratch nowadays) being among the most popular.

And Yggdrasil, the first CDROM-based distribution, is also a *perfect*
counterexample to your "you can't charge for freeware!" assertion.  

If you're going to call someone a liar, at least get your facts right.

